I am used to ____ the rowing boat.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The word **am** changes the construction: this is familiarity, not a former habit.","explanation":"In **am used to**, to is a preposition, so the following verb is **repairing**.","keywords":["accustomed-state"],"skill":"accustomed-state","contextKey":"past-habits-50","fingerprint":"5b6cbd42f17532ebb8ff4508e5c43bbe8bcf21d24c53be6c6aad721a1eafaa51","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-habits"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The word **am** changes the construction: this is familiarity, not a former habit.","In **am used to**, to is a preposition, so the following verb is **repairing**.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In **am used to**, to is a preposition, so the following verb is **repairing**.","B":"The base verb belongs in the former-habit pattern, not after am used to.","C":"A past participle does not name the activity after this preposition.","D":"Do not put a to-infinitive after the preposition to in this pattern."}}},{"id":"a898d22d-157e-5695-a0b5-d02b7eed1f9e","topicSlug":"past-habits","level":"B1","prompt":"Recall a repeated action with would: In my old job, I would ____ the rowing boat every week.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The context supplies a past routine; **would** still takes the base form.","explanation":"Would takes the base verb **repair**. The old job and every week establish a repeated past action.","keywords":["repeated-past-action"],"skill":"repeated-past-action","contextKey":"past-habits-50","fingerprint":"aa82455f8879e56b83b09f82c5a9bb4abef5658b129535e6afb184e43517fd3e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-habits"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The context supplies a past routine; **would** still takes the base form.","Would takes the base verb **repair**. The old job and every week establish a repeated past action.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Would alone cannot take an -ing form.","B":"Would takes a base verb, not a past form.","C":"Would takes a bare infinitive without to.","D":"Would takes the base verb **repair**. The old job and every week establish a repeated past action."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Distinguish a former habit from a familiar experience and from the process of adapting.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Former habits and states","explanation":"Use **used to + base verb** for a past habit or state that contrasts with now. Used to can describe actions and states. In standard written questions and negatives after did, use **use to**.","pattern":"I **used to live** here. / Did you **use to live** here?","commonMistakes":["Used to is not a normal present-habit form. For habits now, use present simple."],"examples":["Complete a former habit: I used to **prepare** the visitor guide, but I do not do that now.","Complete a former habit: I used to **restore** the old portrait, but I do not do that now."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Repeated past actions with would","explanation":"Would can recall repeated actions within an established past period. It normally needs a clear past setting and does not usually replace used to for a continuing state such as owning a house.","pattern":"When we were children, we **would visit** every summer.","commonMistakes":["We would own a cottage does not normally describe simple past ownership."],"examples":["Use did in the negative: I did not **use to prepare** the visitor guide in my old job.","Use did in the negative: I did not **use to restore** the old portrait in my old job."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Being accustomed to something","explanation":"In **be used to**, used is an adjective and to is a preposition. Follow it with a noun or an -ing form. It means familiar or accustomed, and be can refer to present, past or future.","pattern":"She **is used to working** at night.","commonMistakes":["Is used to work does not mean that she is accustomed to working."],"examples":["I am familiar with the task: I am used to **preparing** the visitor guide.","I am familiar with the task: I am used to **restoring** the old portrait."],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"The process of adapting","explanation":"**Get used to + noun / -ing** describes becoming accustomed. The tense belongs to get: am getting used to, got used to, will get used to. This is distinct from a discontinued habit.","pattern":"I’m **getting used to driving** on the left.","commonMistakes":["Do not add an extra be after get; get used to already expresses the change."],"examples":["Recall a repeated action with would: In my old job, I would **prepare** the visitor guide every week.","Recall a repeated action with would: In my old job, I would **restore** the old portrait every week."],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["I **used to live** here. / Did you **use to live** here?","When we were children, we **would visit** every summer.","She **is used to working** at night.","I’m **getting used to driving** on the left."]},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["future-perfect-continuous"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
